The problem:
  - We want to create faster, more functional websites.
    we're trying to do more and more with Javascript.
    - Offload tasks from the server to the web browser.
        You're not paying for the cpu or bandwidth.
    - Better user experience:
        more responsive,
        non-web things like drag-and-drop.

  - BUT: Javascript is a pain
    (picture of a messed up website on IE)
    - browser incompatibilities
      - IE is the big stinker here.
        - Most of us can't afford to just ignore it.
        - There are more and more browsers out there.
          - You can't just support IE, Firefox, and maybe Safari.
            - Now there is iPhone, iPad, Android, other tablets....

    - Ajax is tricker: asynchronous, lots of typing, callbacks.

jQuery:
  - history
  - 1.4.2 was huge. Now we're up to 1.4.4.

Philosophy:

  Hide browser differences:
    - Don't call native javascript functions (especially DOM stuff),
      but use jQuery calls to wrap browser-testing for you.
    - Use jQuery events, not native events.
    - If really necessary, test for functionality, not browser:
      - jQuery gives you lots of flags for this kind of thing.
      - It's kind of the autoconf method, if you have ever used that before.

  Graceful degradation:
    - People should be able to use your site even if all the JS doesn't work.
      - Even Grandma can use the site.
    - More and more people are abandoning this principle:
      - "web applications": Gmail, Google maps, ...
      - others, too: Stack Overflow (kind of).
      - I'm sympathetic.
        - "Grandma" isn't the one disabling javascript.
        - It takes a lot of work the build your site twice.
        - Javascript and Ajax really do enable better usability.
        - But if you consider mobile devices, there are a lot of crippled browsers.
        - And Grandma might be running IE 5 or 6.
        - So it's still pretty important to aim for graceful degregation.

  Unobtrusive Javascript (UJS):
    Bad: <a href="javascript:alert('Thanks for clicking me!');">click here</a>
    What if the user doesn't have javascript?
    That's messy! Do you really want to read that?

  Select items in the DOM with CSS syntax:
    // Select based on the element name:
    p {
      margin-top: 0.8em;
    }

    // Select based on the element's "class" attribute:
    .important {
      border: solid red 2px;
    }

    // Select based on the element's "id" attribute:
    #header {
      font-family: Impact;
      font-size: 150%;
    }

    // Combine them:
    ul.todo {
      background-color: #aaeeff;
    }

    // Nest them:
    #header a {
      color: inherit;
    }

  Everything is a callback:
    Who has C experience?
      So what does this mean?:
        typedef void (*sighandler_t)(int);
        sighandler_t signal(int signum, sighandler_t handler);
        If you want to call the `signal` function, what are its two arguments?
    Callbacks are when you provide a method and ask your framework to call it under certain conditions. It's a little circuitous, but it's sometimes useful. Event-based programming: very important for desktop apps or mobile apps. Also the core idea of node.js. Very important to jQuery.
    <script type="javascript">
    function highlight() {
      // ...
    }
    </script>
    <a onmouseover="javascript:highlight">Click here</a>
    This is a simple event-based callback.

  
Important Javascript features for using jQuery:
  Functions are objects:
    They can be arguments to other functions:
      setTimeout(explode, 10000);

      or (an anonymous function)

      setTimeout(function() {
        alert("Time's up!");
      }, 10000);
    They can have their own sub-functions:
    Here is one with a helper function.
    Note how it can read the state of its parent function:
      function fibonacci(n) {
        // Optimization of fibonacci(n-2) + fibonacci(n-1)
        var results = [1, 1];
        var _fib = function(n) {
          if (!results[n]) {
            results[n] = _fib(n-2) + _fib(n-1);
          }
          return results[n];
        }
        return _fib(n);
      }
    You can use this to create "objects" where the outside function is the constructor:
      function Dog(theName) {
        var name = theName;
        this.speak = function() {
          alert(name + " says \"Woof!\"");
        }
        this.fetch = function () {
          // ...
        }
      }

      var fido = new Dog("Fido");
      fido.speak();
  
  Single-threaded:
    Ajax adds asynchronous calls on the network, but only one thread in Javascript.
    Much simpler!
    If you do something

  Event-based:
    The DOM has all kinds of events, for clicking, mousing, etc.

First Problem: Run some JS after the page is fully loaded:
  - Necessary for UJS
    - We want to serve the basic HTML first, then use Javascript to enhance it.
  - You can't reliably access anything in the DOM before the page is loaded.
  - We could say this:
    window.onload = function() {
      // ...
    }

    But that won't run until all the pictures are loaded, too.
    Pictures, and YouTube videos, and ....

  - Here is the jQuery:
    $(function() {
      alert("Hello, world!");
    });
  - "$" is a valid identifier in Javascript.
  - So what is it here?
    - We're calling a function named "$".
    - We're passing an anonymous function.
    - "$" is /the/ jQuery function.
      - Its behavior varies based on what you pass it.
      - If you pass it a function, then it runs that function
        when the page is fully-loaded. (This is called a ready-handler.)
      - It can other things, too, as we'll see.
      - You can also call it jQuery, instead of $, to avoid name conflicts.
      - Or you can do this:
        (function($) {
          // Use "$" here....
        })(jQuery);
  - Another example:
    function show_login_box() {
      if (!user_is_logged_in) {
        // ...
      }
    }
    $(show_login_box);
    
    or
      - remember functions are objects.
      - What do you do with objects? You assign them to variables!

    var show_login_box = function() {
      if (!user_is_logged_in) {
        // ...
      }
    }
    $(show_login_box);


Second Problem: Manipulate the DOM:
  - Really there are two parts: picking out the elements we care about,
    then doing something to them.
  - jQuery lets us query the DOM for the elements we want,
    which makes UJS easy.
    - We reach into the DOM and add Javascript wherever we like.
      - If the user doesn't have Javascript, they still get the unadorned HTML.

  - jQuery's DOM selection uses a CSS-like syntax,
    and we get back a "matched set" object, representing those elements.
    Then we can call methods on the matched set to manipulate the elements' properties.
    So we make all our main images fade in:
    $('#body img').fadeIn();

    Here we're asking for all the img tags in our main body of text,
    and we're making them fade in.
    The first part is the selection; the second part is the action.
    The selection uses that dollar sign again! But we're passing a string,
    not a function, so jQuery interprets it differently.
    This has nothing to do with ready handlers.
    Instead jQuery is going to search the DOM for things that match our expression,
    then return a "matched set" object.
    We can call methods on that object to affect the elements in the set.
    In this case, we're making the images fade in.

    Or change the html in something:
    $('#messages').html(get_latest_tweets());

    Or add a child:
    $('ul#todo').append("<li>Buy groceries</li>");

    The sector string is very powerful. Actually it supports more than just CSS:
    $('tr:last-child').addClass('table-bottom');
    
    Find all links with an absolute URL:
    $("a[href^='http://']").addClass('absolute-link');

    You can also pass another matched set as a second parameter,
    to restrict the search to within that set:
      var $game = $('#game');
      function kill_all_monsters() {
        $('div.monster', $game).remove();
      }

    These methods generally return the matched set again, so they are chainable:
    $('#status').removeClass('red').removeClass('yellow').addClass('green');

    Some of the methods available:

    html
    text
    append
    prepend
    appendTo
    preprendTo
    before
    after
    insertBefore
    insertAfter

    wrap
    wrapAll
    wrapInner
    unwrap
    remove
    detach
    empty
    clone
    replaceWith
    replaceAll
    val

    addClass
    removeClass
    hasClass
    attr
    css
    offset
    position
    width
    height
    data
    removeData

    show
    hide
    toggle
    fadeIn
    fadeOut
    fadeTo
    slideDown
    slideUp

  - If the built-in methods don't give you what you need, you can use each:
  - We want to add syntax highlighting to our slideshow:
    $(function() {
      $('code').each(function() {
        parse_and_add_color(this);
        $(this).css('border', 'dashed #aaeeff 1px');
      });
    });
    - The function gets called once for each item in the matched set.
      - jQuery sets `this` to the DOM object for that iteration.
      - You can say `$(this)` to turn any DOM object into a matched set.

